       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Acetaminophen 2000 mg three times daily comes to 6 g/day. That is above the usual adult maximum of 4 g/day (many institutions cap at 3 g/day), and it risks hepatotoxicity. A single 2000 mg dose is also higher than the standard maximum single dose of 1000 mg. Your patient's normal liver function and 70 kg weight don't make this dose safe.\n\nYou may have meant one of these:\n- **1000 mg by mouth three times daily** (3 g/day), a common conservative regimen.\n- **1000 mg by mouth four times daily** (4 g/day), which is the ceiling.\n- **650 mg by mouth every 6 hours** (2.6 g/day).\n- **2000 mg per day in total**, for example 1000 mg twice daily, if the 2000 mg was meant as a daily total.\n\nTell me which regimen you want, or give me a different dose, and I'll enter it right away. Please also review the order with your resident or attending. Also check that the patient isn't getting acetaminophen from other sources, such as combination products, that would count toward the daily total."
      }
